// The token kinds. Each is a zero-action named function so that no bare literal
// kind number ever appears at a call site.
//
// They are written as `(import lex_kbase) + n` rather than as plain literals for a
// reason the language forces: two functions with the same signature whose bodies
// differ only in the names of their own locals are a compile error, and a
// zero-action function returning a bare integer is *only* distinguished by that
// integer. `ast_k_nil() { } return int 100;` in another module would collide with
// any function here that happened to return 100. Naming the base makes the callee
// part of every body's fingerprint, so a kind family can never collide with
// another family, and the family's origin lives in exactly one place.

lex_k_eof() {
  int v = (import lex_kbase) + 0;
} return int v;

lex_k_ident() {
  int v = (import lex_kbase) + 1;
} return int v;
